WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.428 --> 00:00:02.246
When contact goes unnoticed,

2
00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.627
a routine placement can tip a cup.

3
00:00:04.942 --> 00:00:06.535
In these example trials,

4
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:10.575
CoPRE retreats, while the neural baseline keeps moving.

5
00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:13.787
We present CoPRE:

6
00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:17.142
Contact-free Proprioceptive Response Estimation.

7
00:00:18.391 --> 00:00:20.272
Proprioceptive feedback provides

8
00:00:20.272 --> 00:00:22.774
joint states and motor torque estimates.

9
00:00:23.088 --> 00:00:26.049
CoPRE learns the contact-free torque reference

10
00:00:26.049 --> 00:00:28.985
and detects contact-induced deviations.

11
00:00:30.424 --> 00:00:31.387
For each robot,

12
00:00:31.569 --> 00:00:34.913
we fit the nominal torque predictor on training recordings

13
00:00:35.043 --> 00:00:36.911
and set the detection threshold

14
00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:38.926
on separate calibration recordings.

15
00:00:40.244 --> 00:00:42.456
State exclusion keeps recent,

16
00:00:42.586 --> 00:00:46.416
potentially contact-affected observations out of the predictor.

17
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.783
Earlier states and known commands

18
00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:51.377
provide the nominal joint torque reference.

19
00:00:52.231 --> 00:00:55.588
Torque residuals measure observed minus predicted torque.

20
00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:57.968
Noise-weighted Jacobian aggregation

21
00:00:57.968 --> 00:01:00.509
combines them into a contact score.

22
00:01:00.876 --> 00:01:02.139
Three consecutive scores

23
00:01:02.139 --> 00:01:04.838
at or above threshold confirm contact.

24
00:01:05.878 --> 00:01:08.327
We measure reference sliding resistance:

25
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.327
the force needed to start each book stack sliding.

26
00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:16.965
On ARX, CoPRE retains sensitivity

27
00:01:16.965 --> 00:01:18.572
under combined calibration.

28
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.006
Both baselines miss all contacts.

29
00:01:21.884 --> 00:01:25.570
The Unitree G1 right arm shows the same advantage,

30
00:01:25.674 --> 00:01:28.136
with its own predictor and calibration.

31
00:01:29.826 --> 00:01:31.828
Across all tested conditions,

32
00:01:31.931 --> 00:01:36.262
trial recall reaches seventy four point one percent on ARX

33
00:01:36.418 --> 00:01:39.170
and eighty two point two percent on G1.

34
00:01:39.484 --> 00:01:43.420
Comparisons use shared calibration rules within each robot.

35
00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:47.360
CoPRE reaches F ninety at reference resistances

36
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.006
of three point five newtons on ARX

37
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.059
and five point five on G1.

38
00:01:54.238 --> 00:01:55.765
We integrate CoPRE

39
00:01:55.765 --> 00:01:59.648
into belief-guided manipulation planning for object placement.

40
00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:03.423
Detected contact triggers retreat and replanning.

41
00:02:05.397 --> 00:02:07.688
The planner selects an alternate placement,

42
00:02:07.857 --> 00:02:10.043
keeping the cup upright in this trial.

43
00:02:13.429 --> 00:02:14.483
For book insertion,

44
00:02:14.692 --> 00:02:17.365
a neighboring book blocks the initial approach.

45
00:02:20.029 --> 00:02:22.768
This contact updates the spatial belief map.

46
00:02:25.387 --> 00:02:27.348
The planner ranks candidate poses

47
00:02:27.348 --> 00:02:31.165
by feasibility, goal distance, and previous failures.

48
00:02:34.526 --> 00:02:37.620
It selects a revised approach that clears the obstruction.

49
00:02:37.948 --> 00:02:40.502
The robot then advances the book into the gap

50
00:02:40.632 --> 00:02:42.054
and seats it on the shelf.

51
00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:47.945
The gripper releases the book and withdraws.

52
00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:54.193
These results demonstrate improved weak-contact sensitivity

53
00:02:54.193 --> 00:02:55.998
on two robot platforms,

54
00:02:56.207 --> 00:02:59.011
and its use in belief-guided manipulation planning.
